# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Valve Three Opens After the Clap

**Story & Context:** During an early pressure-table rehearsal, Nadia discovers that valve position three gives the hydraulophone enough resistance to produce stable bends without losing the splash transients around each note. Youssef builds the main groove so that the valve is opened only after a two-clap cue at the end of every fourth cycle.

Mara answers the claps with one clipped snare flam, while Samir uses a foam-muted baritone string as a low knock beneath the first hydraulophone note. The track begins with hand percussion and dry snare only. Full water pressure arrives after sixteen bars, making the melody feel physically larger without adding harmonic layers.

At the peak, Nadia partially closes one jet during a sustained note and lets the pitch collapse downward. The ensemble follows the pressure drop instead of staying locked to the original tonal center. The final section returns to valve three, but the groove never regains its earlier density.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Stainless pressure valve marked 3 beside cobalt tile, two wet handprints near a hydraulophone jet, bendir and field-snare sticks on the concrete, prepared baritone guitar reflected in shallow water. Spoken fragments limited to “two claps,” “open three,” “hold the pressure,” and “let it fall.”

## 2. Snare Line Across the Runoff Channel

**Story & Context:** Mara notices that the narrow runoff channel beside the rehearsal court creates a sharp reflection whenever she plays a rimshot facing across it. She develops a straight sixteenth-note snare figure that deliberately opposes Youssef’s rolling bendir accents.

Nadia plays short hydraulophone staccatos rather than long bends, matching the snare’s geometric character. Samir prepares two baritone strings with paper and metal clips, creating one dull knock and one buzzing response. The groove alternates between sections dominated by hand-drum swing and sections where the field snare becomes the primary clock.

In the bridge, Mara stops entirely while the other musicians preserve the exact spaces where the snare line had been. When she returns, she plays only the final note of each former phrase, reducing the rigid grid to a structural memory.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Narrow tiled runoff channel dividing wet concrete, field snare placed on one side and hydraulophone jets visible on the other, paper-prepared baritone strings in close focus, bendir leaning against a stainless rail. Spoken fragments limited to “across the channel,” “keep it straight,” “leave the line empty,” and “only the last hit.”

## 3. Pressure Falls Before the Brass Breath

**Story & Context:** During a late session, the main pump briefly loses pressure just before a guest euphonium player begins a low note. The accidental sequence becomes the structure of the EP closer: water pressure must fall first, then brass breath enters into the temporary empty space.

Youssef strips the groove to bendir, clap, and one compact kick. Mara uses brushes on a heavily damped snare instead of rimshots, while Samir plays slow prepared-baritone dyads with almost no sustain. Nadia begins with full-flow melodic lines but gradually closes the supply until only intermittent pitched jets remain.

When the water nearly stops, the euphonium enters with one breath-led low note slightly below the modal center. Nadia reopens the main valve slowly beneath it. The final sound is not a drum hit but the low mechanical thump of the pump reaching stable pressure again.

